1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,557
This is ECE 358, video 18. The topic is link-state routing: Dijkstra's algorithm and OSPF.

2
00:00:10,007 --> 00:00:28,631
After this video, you can run Dijkstra's algorithm and fill its table step by step. You can draw the least-cost-path tree and the forwarding table. You can explain link state, OSPF, flooding and link costs. And you can solve question 85 of the question bank.

3
00:00:29,081 --> 00:00:43,460
Routing finds good paths through a network of routers. We use the graph model of slide 79. Each node is a router. Each edge is a link. The number on an edge is the link cost, c x y.

4
00:00:43,910 --> 00:00:58,118
A path is a sequence of links from a source to a destination. The cost of a path is the sum of its link costs. For example, the path u, x, y, z costs 1 plus 1 plus 2, which is 4.

5
00:00:58,568 --> 00:01:06,845
Another path from u to z goes through w. It costs 5 plus 5, which is 10. So the first path is better.

6
00:01:07,295 --> 00:01:22,527
A good path is a least-cost path. The routing algorithm in u must find the least-cost path from u to every other node. These paths give the forwarding table of u. The green links show the answer that we will compute.

7
00:01:22,977 --> 00:01:40,044
Slide 82 describes Dijkstra's link-state algorithm. It is centralized. Every node knows the whole topology and every link cost. The purple chips show that each router holds the same map. A link state broadcast gives them this map.

8
00:01:40,494 --> 00:01:49,027
Because all nodes have the same map, each node can run the algorithm alone. No node waits for the results of other nodes.

9
00:01:49,477 --> 00:02:01,275
The algorithm computes the least-cost paths from one node, the source, to all other nodes. Here the source is u. The result gives the forwarding table of that source node.

10
00:02:01,725 --> 00:02:14,098
The algorithm is iterative. After k iterations, it knows the least-cost path to k destinations. In the picture, three nodes are done. They are in the set N prime.

11
00:02:14,548 --> 00:02:36,585
Here is the notation. c x y is the direct link cost from x to y. It is infinity when x and y are not neighbors. D of v is the current estimate of the least cost from the source to v. p of v is the node before v on that path. N prime is the set of nodes whose least cost is definitely known.

12
00:02:37,035 --> 00:02:57,409
Slide 83 gives the algorithm. Lines 1 to 6 are the initialization. N prime holds only the source u. For each neighbor v of u, D of v is the direct cost c u v. For every other node, D of v is infinity. This first estimate may not be the minimum cost.

13
00:02:57,859 --> 00:03:08,099
Then the loop starts. Line 9 finds the node w that is not in N prime and has the smallest D. Here it is x, with D equal to 1.

14
00:03:08,549 --> 00:03:21,605
Line 10 adds w to N prime. The cost of x is now final. No other path to x can be cheaper, because every other path already costs at least 1 before its next link.

15
00:03:22,055 --> 00:03:39,399
Lines 11 and 12 update the neighbors of w that are not in N prime. For each such v, D of v becomes the minimum of the old D of v, and D of w plus c w v. The purple links show the neighbors that x checks.

16
00:03:39,849 --> 00:03:52,606
Line 15 repeats the loop until all nodes are in N prime. Each pass adds one node. So for n nodes, the loop runs n minus 1 times after the initialization.

17
00:03:53,056 --> 00:04:03,467
Here is the update rule of line 12 in a small picture. At the start, u knows only the direct link to w. So D of w is 5.

18
00:04:03,917 --> 00:04:09,015
Then x joins N prime. Its cost, D of x, is 1, and it is final.

19
00:04:09,465 --> 00:04:17,935
Now a path through x is possible. Its cost is D of x plus c x w. That is 1 plus 3, which is 4.

20
00:04:18,385 --> 00:04:34,726
The minimum of 5 and 4 is 4. So D of w becomes 4, and the predecessor p of w becomes x. Change D and p only when the new cost is strictly smaller. If the costs are equal, keep the old entry.

21
00:04:35,176 --> 00:04:55,784
Now the example of slide 84. The source is u. Step 0 is the initialization. The neighbors of u are v, w and x. So D of v is 2, D of w is 5, and D of x is 1, all with predecessor u. y and z are not neighbors, so their D is infinity.

22
00:04:56,234 --> 00:05:06,879
Step 1. The smallest D outside N prime is 1, for node x. So x joins N prime. The green circle in row 0 marks this choice.

23
00:05:07,329 --> 00:05:31,223
Now update the neighbors of x that are not in N prime: v, w and y. For v: 1 plus 2 is 3. That is not smaller than 2, so v keeps 2 comma u. For w: 1 plus 3 is 4, smaller than 5. So w becomes 4 comma x. For y: 1 plus 1 is 2, smaller than infinity. So y becomes 2 comma x.

24
00:05:31,673 --> 00:05:43,769
Step 2. Now v and y both have D equal to 2. This is a tie. The slide picks y. Ties can be broken in any order. The final costs do not change.

25
00:05:44,219 --> 00:06:00,261
y joins N prime. Update its neighbors w and z. For w: 2 plus 1 is 3, smaller than 4. So w becomes 3 comma y. For z: 2 plus 2 is 4. So z becomes 4 comma y.

26
00:06:00,711 --> 00:06:14,429
Step 3. The smallest D is 2, for v. v joins N prime. Its only neighbor outside N prime is w. 2 plus 3 is 5, not smaller than 3. So nothing changes.

27
00:06:14,879 --> 00:06:28,447
Step 4. The smallest D is 3, for w. w joins N prime. Its neighbor z gives 3 plus 5, which is 8. That is not smaller than 4. So z keeps 4 comma y.

28
00:06:28,897 --> 00:06:45,451
Step 5. z joins N prime with cost 4. All nodes are in N prime, so the algorithm stops. The final costs are: v 2, w 3, x 1, y 2, and z 4. The green links are the predecessors.

29
00:06:45,901 --> 00:07:01,411
Now build the least-cost-path tree. Trace the predecessors back from each node. For z: p of z is y, p of y is x, and p of x is u. So the path from u to z is u, x, y, z, with cost 4.

30
00:07:01,861 --> 00:07:07,407
Do this for every node. The green links are the links from each node to its predecessor.

31
00:07:07,857 --> 00:07:15,260
Remove all other links. The result is the least-cost-path tree from u. It has 5 links for 6 nodes.

32
00:07:15,710 --> 00:07:26,739
Now the forwarding table of u. It needs only the first link of each path. For v, the path is u to v directly. So the outgoing link is u v.

33
00:07:27,189 --> 00:07:35,808
Every other destination, x, y, w and z, is in the subtree below x. So all of them use the link u x.

34
00:07:36,258 --> 00:07:46,797
One small error on slide 85: the table lists destination x twice. The last row is for z. It also uses the link u x.

35
00:07:47,247 --> 00:08:05,956
Here is a second example, from slides 86 and 92. The source is again u. Pause the video and try the table first. Step 0: the neighbors of u are v with cost 7, w with cost 3, and x with cost 5. y and z are infinity.

36
00:08:06,406 --> 00:08:28,614
Step 1. The smallest D is 3, for w. w joins N prime. Update its neighbors. v: 3 plus 3 is 6, smaller than 7, so 6 comma w. y: 3 plus 8 is 11, so 11 comma w. x: 3 plus 4 is 7, not smaller than 5, so x keeps 5 comma u.

37
00:08:29,064 --> 00:08:45,256
Step 2. The smallest D is 5, for x. x joins N prime. Update y: 5 plus 7 is 12, not smaller than 11. Update z: 5 plus 9 is 14, so z becomes 14 comma x.

38
00:08:45,706 --> 00:08:57,973
Step 3. The smallest D is 6, for v. v joins N prime. Update y: 6 plus 4 is 10, smaller than 11. So y becomes 10 comma v.

39
00:08:58,423 --> 00:09:10,711
Step 4. The smallest D is 10, for y. y joins N prime. Update z: 10 plus 2 is 12, smaller than 14. So z becomes 12 comma y.

40
00:09:11,161 --> 00:09:23,214
Step 5. z joins N prime with cost 12, and the algorithm stops. The final costs are: v 6, w 3, x 5, y 10, and z 12.

41
00:09:23,664 --> 00:09:32,965
Trace the predecessors to get the tree. p of v is w, p of y is v, p of z is y, and p of w and x is u.

42
00:09:33,415 --> 00:09:38,877
Remove the other links. This is the red tree of slide 92, here in green.

43
00:09:39,327 --> 00:09:53,193
The routing table of u needs the first hop of each path. For z, the path is u, w, v, y, z. So the outgoing link is u w. The same is true for v, y and w.

44
00:09:53,643 --> 00:10:04,502
Only x is reached by the link u x. Slide 93 shows the same table: x uses u x, and all other destinations use u w.

45
00:10:04,952 --> 00:10:14,829
Slide 92 also says: run Dijkstra's algorithm on all other nodes. Each router computes its own tree and its own routing table.

46
00:10:15,279 --> 00:10:41,263
OSPF means Open Shortest Path First. It is the link-state protocol for routing inside one autonomous system. Slide 122 says that it is widely used. In step 1, each router floods OSPF advertisements, also called L S advertisements. So every router learns the complete topology of the network inside the A S.

47
00:10:41,713 --> 00:10:52,636
In step 2, each router, for example u, runs Dijkstra's algorithm on this topology. It finds the shortest paths from itself to all other nodes.

48
00:10:53,086 --> 00:11:01,534
In step 3, each router finds the next hop for each destination from those paths. These next hops fill its routing table.

49
00:11:01,984 --> 00:11:12,523
The link state of a router is the list of its own links and their costs. All routers build the same map from these lists. So their shortest paths agree with each other.

50
00:11:12,973 --> 00:11:22,167
Slide 88 lists three types of links in OSPF. The first is a point-to-point link. It connects two routers directly.

51
00:11:22,617 --> 00:11:34,287
The second is a transient link, also called a broadcast link. It is a LAN that connects two or more routers. Traffic can enter the LAN through one router and leave through another.

52
00:11:34,737 --> 00:11:43,163
The third is a stub link. It is a LAN with only one router. Traffic to or from this LAN always goes through that one router.

53
00:11:43,613 --> 00:11:52,787
A router includes all of these links in its L S advertisement. So the other routers learn which destinations are connected in the A S.

54
00:11:53,237 --> 00:12:06,485
Slide 89 shows the link state of each router in a five-router network. The link state of A lists its own links. A to B with cost 5, A to C with cost 2, and A to D with cost 3.

55
00:12:06,935 --> 00:12:13,313
The link state of B: B to A cost 5, B to C cost 4, and B to E cost 3.

56
00:12:13,763 --> 00:12:19,737
The link state of C: C to A cost 2, C to B cost 4, and C to E cost 4.

57
00:12:20,187 --> 00:12:24,816
D has only one link. Its link state is D to A with cost 3.

58
00:12:25,266 --> 00:12:30,066
The link state of E: E to B cost 3, and E to C cost 4.

59
00:12:30,516 --> 00:12:44,063
Each router collects the link states of all routers. Together, the five lists give the full map. Each link appears twice, once from each end. With this map, each router runs Dijkstra's algorithm.

60
00:12:44,513 --> 00:12:55,329
Slide 90 shows how one link state reaches every router. This is called flooding. Router D creates its advertisement. It holds D, A, 3.

61
00:12:55,779 --> 00:13:02,008
D sends the advertisement on all of its links. D has only one link, so A receives it.

62
00:13:02,458 --> 00:13:07,151
A sends a copy on all of its other links. So B and C receive it.

63
00:13:07,601 --> 00:13:21,916
B and C send copies on their other links, so E receives it. B and C also send copies to each other. A router that already has this advertisement does not send it again. So the flooding stops.

64
00:13:22,366 --> 00:13:30,046
The link state of every other node is flooded in the same way. At the end, every router has the same complete map.

65
00:13:30,496 --> 00:13:48,309
What is the cost of a link? Slide 91 gives the Cisco default. The cost is inversely proportional to the link speed. Cost equals 100 Mbps divided by the link speed. A 1 Mbps link has cost 100.

66
00:13:48,759 --> 00:13:51,639
A 10 Mbps link has cost 10.

67
00:13:52,089 --> 00:14:00,751
A 100 Mbps link has cost 1. So faster links get smaller costs, and Dijkstra's algorithm prefers them.

68
00:14:01,201 --> 00:14:16,006
For a 1 Gbps link, 100 divided by 1000 is point 1. The slide gives cost 1, so the cost does not go below 1. Routers can also be configured with other cost methods.

69
00:14:16,456 --> 00:14:29,619
Now the question bank. Questions 81 to 84 are short descriptive questions. Question 81: what is the main function of OSPF? Pause the video and try it.

70
00:14:30,069 --> 00:14:43,871
OSPF does routing inside one A S. Each router learns the topology by flooding. It runs Dijkstra's algorithm. Then it fills its routing table with the next hop to each destination.

71
00:14:44,321 --> 00:15:00,001
Question 82: what cost metrics can OSPF use? The operator sets the link cost. The default on the slide is 100 Mbps divided by the link speed. Other methods can be configured.

72
00:15:00,451 --> 00:15:15,555
Question 83: explain the link state of a router. It is the list of the router's own links and their costs. For example, the link state of A is A B 5, A C 2, and A D 3. It is flooded to all routers.

73
00:15:16,005 --> 00:15:24,176
Question 84: which graph algorithm does OSPF use? It uses Dijkstra's shortest-path algorithm.

74
00:15:24,626 --> 00:15:35,186
Question 85 of the question bank. Run Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest-path tree rooted at node z. Then build the routing table on z.

75
00:15:35,636 --> 00:15:42,335
This is the same graph as slide 84, but now the source is z. Pause the video and try it.

76
00:15:42,785 --> 00:15:51,105
Step 0. The neighbors of z are w with cost 5, and y with cost 2. u, v and x are infinity.

77
00:15:51,555 --> 00:16:08,984
Step 1. The smallest D is 2, for y. y joins N prime. Update its neighbors. w: 2 plus 1 is 3, smaller than 5. So w becomes 3 comma y. x: 2 plus 1 is 3. So x becomes 3 comma y.

78
00:16:09,434 --> 00:16:32,303
Step 2. w and x both have cost 3. This is a tie. We take w first. The other order gives the same final result. w joins N prime. u: 3 plus 5 is 8, so 8 comma w. v: 3 plus 3 is 6, so 6 comma w. x: 3 plus 3 is 6, not smaller than 3.

79
00:16:32,753 --> 00:16:48,860
Step 3. x joins N prime with cost 3. u: 3 plus 1 is 4, smaller than 8. So u becomes 4 comma x. v: 3 plus 2 is 5, smaller than 6. So v becomes 5 comma x.

80
00:16:49,310 --> 00:16:58,995
Step 4. The smallest D is 4, for u. u joins N prime. v: 4 plus 2 is 6, not smaller than 5. No change.

81
00:16:59,445 --> 00:17:10,048
Step 5. v joins N prime with cost 5. The final costs from z are: u 4, v 5, w 3, x 3, and y 2.

82
00:17:10,498 --> 00:17:19,458
Trace the predecessors. p of u is x, p of v is x, p of x is y, p of w is y, and p of y is z.

83
00:17:19,908 --> 00:17:25,796
Remove the other links. This is the shortest-path tree rooted at z. It has 5 links.

84
00:17:26,246 --> 00:17:35,867
The routing table on z. Every path in the tree starts with the link z y. So every destination uses the outgoing link z y.

85
00:17:36,317 --> 00:17:48,349
Check the surprising row. z has a direct link to w with cost 5. But the path z, y, w costs only 3. So w also uses z y.

86
00:17:48,799 --> 00:18:15,402
Here is the method for an exam question. One: write row 0 from the direct links of the source. Two: pick the smallest D that is not in N prime, and circle it. Three: update only the neighbors of the new node that are not in N prime. Change D and p only when the new cost is strictly smaller. Four: trace the predecessors to get the tree, and take the first link of each path for the table.

87
00:18:15,852 --> 00:18:35,969
Common errors: writing the predecessor as the next hop. In slide 84, p of z is y, but the next hop from u is x. Other errors: updating a node already in N prime, changing p when the costs are equal, and forgetting that a two-hop path can be cheaper than a direct link.

88
00:18:36,419 --> 00:18:43,801
To check your answer, add the link costs along each tree path. The sum must equal the final D of that node.

89
00:18:44,251 --> 00:19:01,211
Here is the recap. In link-state routing, every router knows the full topology and all link costs, from flooded advertisements. Dijkstra's algorithm adds one node to N prime in each step, and updates D of v with the minimum rule.

90
00:19:01,661 --> 00:19:16,999
Trace the predecessors to get the tree. The forwarding table holds the first link of each path. OSPF uses this method inside an A S: flood, run Dijkstra, find the next hop, and fill the routing table.

91
00:19:17,449 --> 00:19:28,116
The default link cost is 100 Mbps divided by the link speed. The next video covers the other routing method, distance vector.
